{"availability":"available","full":"plasma-umass/browsix","name":"browsix","description":"Browsix is a Unix-like operating system for the browser. ","category":"Developer tools","language":"JavaScript","stars":3184,"forks":182,"created":"2016-02-17T16:29:16Z","updated":"2022-03-27T22:12:32Z","license":null,"branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":["browserfs","javascript-framework","unix-like"],"demo":"https://unix.bpowers.net/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T16:05:54.156Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T16:05:54.156Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T16:05:54.156Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T16:05:54.156Z","stars":3184,"forks":182}],"technologies":["Node.js"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["While standard operating systems like Unix make it relatively simple to build complex applications, web browsers lack the features that make this possible. This project is Browsix, a JavaScript-only framework that brings the essence of Unix to the browser. Browsix makes core Unix features available to web applications (including pipes, processes, signals, sockets, and a shared file system) and extends JavaScript runtimes for C, C++, Go, and Node.js programs so they can run in a Unix-like environment within the browser. Browsix also provides a POSIX-like shell that makes it easy to compose…","For more details, check out our tech report (PDF).","Another way to think about this is that modern web applications are multi-process by nature - the client and some of the application logic lives in the browser, and some of it lives in the cloud, often implemented as microservices."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/plasma-umass/browsix/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"11d6f047c731141387c6fb2863c3f124b1de1b755c8cfa76abfe6bf7881b4f98","extractedAt":"2026-10-08T16:05:54.290Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T16:05:54.290Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"While standard operating systems like Unix make it